{
  "0": "For all four datasets, use one SASRec causal Transformer block, preserving its attention heads and feed-forward structure. Reduced depth may limit overfitting on sparse histories and lower training cost. Keep embeddings, input features, history selection/readout, scoring, loss and optimizer fixed. Train one model per category and update the predictor writer to load the matching architecture.",
  "1": "For all four datasets, RMS-normalize each nonpadding item embedding at lookup and candidate scoring, with one jointly learned global scale initialized to the initial embedding RMS. Keep padding zero and retain embedding dimensions and tying. Controlling item-vector magnitudes may improve sparse-item learning, especially in Electronics. Preserve backbone, features, history/readout, scoring formula, loss and optimizer; match training and prediction.",
  "2": "For all four datasets, replace the current readout with a masked weighted mean of the last two nonpadding causal states, using weights 1 and 3 from older to newer. Renormalize for shorter histories. This retains some smoothing while reducing stale-interest dilution, potentially helping Electronics. Apply the same causal pooling at each supervised training position and prediction; preserve history selection, embeddings, backbone, scoring, loss and optimizer.",
  "3": "For all four datasets, replace dot-product compatibility with a diagonal bilinear score: sum_j d_j*h_j*e_j, initializing d to ones and learning it jointly with the single SASRec model. This adds inexpensive dimension-specific compatibility without a separate predictor and may suppress noisy latent dimensions. Preserve embeddings, backbone topology, input features, history/readout, loss and optimizer; use the identical score in training and the generated predictor."
}
